[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mediator Pattern vs. Event Bus for Component Communication"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As software applications grow, managing communication between decoupled components becomes a critical architectural challenge. Two prominent patterns used to solve this problem are the Mediator Pattern and the Event Bus. While both aim to reduce tight coupling, they approach control flow and dependency management in fundamentally different ways."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Mediator Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Mediator pattern defines an object that encapsulates how a set of objects interact. Instead of components communicating directly with one another, they communicate exclusively through the mediator. This promotes loose coupling by keeping objects from referring to each other explicitly, centralizing the coordination logic."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Mediator {
  constructor() {
    this.colleagues = [];
  }
  register(colleague) {
    this.colleagues.push(colleague);
    colleague.mediator = this;
  }
  send(message, sender) {
    this.colleagues.forEach(colleague => {
      if (colleague !== sender) {
        colleague.receive(message);
      }
    });
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Event Bus"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Event Bus implements the publish-subscribe pattern. Components publish events to the bus without knowing who, if anyone, is listening. Conversely, interested components subscribe to specific event channels. This creates a highly decoupled architecture where the sender has zero knowledge of the receivers."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class EventBus {
  constructor() {
    this.subscribers = {};
  }
  subscribe(event, callback) {
    if (!this.subscribers[event]) {
      this.subscribers[event] = [];
    }
    this.subscribers[event].push(callback);
  }
  publish(event, data) {
    if (!this.subscribers[event]) return;
    this.subscribers[event].forEach(callback => callback(data));
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Architectural Differences"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Coupling: Mediator tightly couples components to the mediator instance, whereas Event Bus completely decouples publishers and subscribers."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Workflow Control: Mediator centralizes business logic and workflow orchestration; Event Bus simply distributes messages blindly."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Debugging: Mediator makes tracing requests easier because the logic passes through a single point. Event Bus can make debugging harder due to implicit, asynchronous flows."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Which Pattern"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose the Mediator Pattern when you have a complex UI workflow, form validation rules, or tightly controlled interactions where a central controller simplifies the logic. Choose an Event Bus when you need to broadcast system-wide notifications, telemetry data, or decoupled component updates across disparate modules."}]
